===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|slepen}}, from Anglian {{inh|en|ang|slēpan}}, a variant of {{m|ang|slǣpan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*slāpan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*slēpaną}}.

====Verb====
[[Image:A child sleeping.jpg|thumb|A sleeping child]]
{{en-verb|sleeps|sleeping|slept}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[rest]] in a state of [[reduce]]d [[consciousness]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:sleep}}
#: {{ux|en|You should '''sleep''' eight hours a day.}}
#* '''2016''', [https://web.archive.org/web/20170930001420/https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/lets-learn-english-lesson-5-where-are-you/3168971.html VOA Learning English] (public domain)
#*: We '''sleep''' in the bedroom.
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|euphemism}} To have [[sexual intercourse]] (see [[sleep with]]).
#: {{ux|en|Last night we '''slept''' together for the first time.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[accommodate]] in beds.
#: {{ux|en|This caravan can '''sleep''' four people comfortably.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic}} To be careless, inattentive, or unconcerned; not to be vigilant; to live thoughtlessly.
#* {{RQ:Atterbury Fourteen Sermons|chapter=A Sermon Preach'd in the Guild-Hall Chapel, London, Sept. 28. 1706. Being the Day of the Election of the Right Honourable the Lord Mayor.|date=28 September 1706|page=407|passage=We '''ſleep''' over our Happineſs, Great as it is, and want to be rous'd into a quick and thankful ſenſe of it, either by an actual Change of Circumſtances, or by a Compariſon of our Own caſe with that of other Men.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|euphemistic|idiomatic}} To be dead.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|1 Thessalonians|4|14|page=1445|column=2|passage=For if we beleeue that Ieſus died, and roſe againe: euen ſo them alſo which '''ſleepe''' in Ieſus, will God bring with him.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=It was that of a man in advanced life, with a long grizzled beard, and also robed in white, probably the husband of the lady, who, after surviving her many years, came at the last to '''sleep''' once more for good and all beside her.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be, or appear to be, in repose; to be quiet; to be unemployed, unused, or unagitated; to rest; to lie dormant.
#: {{ux|en|a question '''sleeps''' for the present; the law '''sleeps'''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|act=V|scene=i|page=182|column=2|passage=How ſweet the moone-light '''ſleepes''' vpon this banke,{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|computing|intransitive}} To [[wait]] for a period of time without performing any action.
#: {{ux|en|After a failed connection attempt, the program '''sleeps''' for 5 seconds before trying again.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|transitive}} To place into a state of [[hibernation]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=Mike Lee; Scott Meyers|title=Learn Mac OS X Snow Leopard|page=91
|passage=Even when you have reasons not to '''sleep''' the computer, it's still a good idea to '''sleep''' the display after a period of time.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|mechanics|dynamics}} To spin on its axis with no other perceptible motion.
#: {{ux|en|When a top is '''sleeping''', it is spinning but not [[precessing]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|author=Anne E. Baker|title=Glossary of Northamptonshire Words and Phrases|passage=''A top '''sleeps''' when it moves with such velocity, and spins so smoothly, that its motion is imperceptible.''}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|mechanics|dynamics}} To cause (a [[spinning top]] or [[yo-yo]]) to spin on its axis with no other perceptible motion.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1995|title=All Aboard for Space: Introducing Space to Youngsters|page=158
|passage=Yo-yo tricks involving '''sleeping''' the yo-yo (like "walking the dog" and "rocking the baby") cannot be performed in space.}}

